  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 315 AM CDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nAn area of stratus clouds across the Northland will slowly scatter \nand move out of the area today, with a fairly calm, nondescript day \non tap. \n\nThursday, an upper level cut off low over the 4 Corners region will \nbe pulled up into an upper level trough coming down from the Canadian \nPrairies. This is expected to generate a couple different areas of \nrainfall across the Midwest and Great Lakes regions. The first drops \nof rain should start to arrive through Thursday morning for the \nNorthland, along a frontogenesis band. Through Thursday afternoon \nand evening, we should see the attending surface low pressure system \nmove from southwest to northeast across Wisconsin and into the Great \nLakes. This is expected to primarily bring rain to portions of NW WI \nin our CWA, with some light rain creeping into Minnesota. The \ngeneral track of this system has trended back north in recent model \nruns, increasing the potential for a tenth to a quarter inch of rain \nor more in our Wisconsin counties. Into Friday morning, wrap-around \nprecipitation following the aforementioned frontal area will push \nacross the region from the more northern upper level disturbance. \nThis will come with a good push of cold air and strong winds. \nPortions of the Arrowhead could see some snowflakes mix in, and wind \ngusts of 25-35mph are possible across the region. 850mb temperatures \nbelow 0C will lead to a large temperature gradient between Lake \nSuperior water and near-surface air, likely leading to some lake \nenhanced precipitation Friday morning and afternoon on the South \nShore as the system pushes east. Some of this lake effect could also \nhave a couple snowflakes mix in. \n\nThose northwest winds should diminish through the day Friday, with \nincreasing high pressure pushing out any precipitation chances for \nthe weekend. Saturday, that high pressure should bring in plentiful \ndry air, along with some gusty southwest winds. A combination of \nafternoon relative humidity dropping to near 25% and wind gusts of \n20-30mph could lead to some near-critical to critical fire weather \nconditions. \n\nAn upper level shortwave passes to our north Sunday, but looks to \nmainly be a dry cold front for our area. Ridging appears to be the \npredominant pattern driver for next week, but a couple shortwaves \nmight be able to sneak us some rainfall at times, though none are \ncertain at this point.",
